Text:
A Desk models a 'desk': an operating system with the file systems it mounts.
Id, Segment, File and Other have no GrammarLine.
An Id models a folder of the desk, a File models a file of the desk, and an Other models anything else that a folder of the desk can hold, such as a symbolic link.
A Segment models the name that a folder of the desk gives to one thing it holds.
The 'root ids' of a Desk are the Ids before the ";".
Desk.multiply(DeskPath Segment) has no Notation: each desk defines it.
Desk.deskPath(TaggedText) has no Notation: each desk defines it.
os$currentDesk() has no Define: os is the operating system, and os$currentDesk() returns the Desk that models the desk at that moment.

Grammar:
Desk ::= Ids ";" Folders
Ids ::= Id1 .. Idn
Folders ::= Folder1 .. Foldern
Folder ::= Id "{" Entries "}"
Entries ::= Entry1 .. Entryn
Entry ::= Segment "->" Element
Element ::= Id | File | Other
DeskPath ::= Id Segment1 .. Segmentn

Well formedness:
in Desk: if Desk is Ids ";" Id1 "{" Entries1 "}" .. Idn "{" Entriesn "}", then Id1 .. Idn are disjoint
in Desk: if Desk is Ids ";" Id1 "{" Entries1 "}" .. Idn "{" Entriesn "}", then Ids subseteq of Id1 .. Idn
in Desk: if Desk is Ids ";" Id1 "{" Entries1 "}" .. Idn "{" Entriesn "}" and Entriesi is Entries Segment "->" Id Entries', then Id is one of Id1 .. Idn
in Entries: if Entries is Segment1 "->" Element1 .. Segmentn "->" Elementn, then Segment1 .. Segmentn are disjoint

Notation: Desk.at(Id) = Id where:
- Desk = Ids Id Ids' ";" Folders

Notation: Desk.at(DeskPath Segment) = Element where:
- Desk.at(DeskPath) = Id
- Desk = Ids ";" Folders Id "{" Entries Segment "->" Element Entries' "}" Folders'

Notation: Desk.at(TaggedText) = Desk.at(Desk.deskPath(TaggedText))

Notation: Desk[Id Segment+=Element] = \
    Ids ";" Folders Id "{" Entries Segment "->" Element "}" Folders' where:
- Desk = Ids ";" Folders Id "{" Entries "}" Folders'

Notation: Desk.add(DeskPath Segment, Element) = Desk[Id1 Segment1+=Element] .. [Idn Segmentn+=Element] where:
- Desk.multiply(DeskPath Segment) = Id1 Segment1 .. Idn Segmentn

Notation: Desk.writeShows(DeskPath, DeskPath') where:
- Desk.at(DeskPath) = Id
- Desk.at(DeskPath') = Id'
- Desk.at(DeskPath' Segment') is undefined
- Desk.add(DeskPath Segment, File).at(DeskPath' Segment') = File

Notation: Desk.same(DeskPath, DeskPath') where:
- Desk.writeShows(DeskPath, DeskPath') or Desk.writeShows(DeskPath', DeskPath)

Notation: Desk.sameOrInside(DeskPath, DeskPath') where:
- Desk.at(DeskPath) = Id
- Desk.at(DeskPath') = Id'
- Desk.same(DeskPath, DeskPath' Segment1 .. Segmentn)

Notation: Desk.sameOrInside(TaggedText, TaggedText') where:
- Desk.sameOrInside(Desk.deskPath(TaggedText), Desk.deskPath(TaggedText'))

Notation: Desk.anyIsSameOrInside(TaggedText, TaggedTexts) where:
- TaggedText' in TaggedTexts
- Desk.sameOrInside(TaggedText, TaggedText') or Desk.sameOrInside(TaggedText', TaggedText)

Implementation notes:
The Notations of this document specify a behaviour; the Notations are not a suggested implementation. An implementation decides Desk.same(DeskPath, DeskPath') with any check that gives the same answer on its operating system and file system; the implementation does not need to add a File.
A program uses same to know that a write through one path changes nothing through another path.
A folder of a desk can show different entries through different paths. Three examples: a union mount has a 'merged' folder that shows the entries of two other folders, the 'lower' folder and the 'upper' folder, and an add through the path of the merged folder lands only in the upper folder; a share shows only the entries whose name passes a filter; an operating system redirects an add in a protected folder to a second folder.
In a Desk, each of those folders has its own Id, and Desk.multiply(DeskPath Segment) says which of those folders get a new entry.
For that reason same looks in both directions: with a union mount, an add through the path of the lower folder shows through the path of the merged folder, and an add through the path of the merged folder does not show through the path of the lower folder.
same is not transitive: the merged folder of a union mount is same with the lower folder and with the upper folder, and the lower folder is not same with the upper folder.
writeShows asks for one Segment and one File, of all the possible ones. When no Segment and no File make an add through one path show through the other path, treating the two paths as not same is safe. This covers two paths that hide every entry from each other, and two paths that refuse every add.
In writeShows, the new entry of the folder at DeskPath' can have a Segment' different from Segment: some file systems change the name of a new entry. The line "Desk.at(DeskPath' Segment') is undefined" makes the entry a new one: a File that the folder at DeskPath' held before the add, with any name, does not count.
Desk.multiply(DeskPath Segment) is empty only when the desk refuses because of what the Desk is, for example a folder that no program can write. A refusal that ends by itself, for example because another program is using the folder, is not a refusal of the model: the implementation tries again.
A desk can show a new entry late, for example on a network file system with a cache. Desk.multiply(DeskPath Segment) lists every folder that gets the new entry, however late; an implementation that adds a File waits as long as the desk needs.
Desk.deskPath(TaggedText) is defined or undefined by the operating system and the text of TaggedText alone: Desk.deskPath(TaggedText) is defined when TaggedText encodes a path that is absolute and has no segment that is empty, "." or "..". Desk.deskPath(TaggedText) is defined also when nothing is at that path, or when the drive of that path is not mounted; then Desk.at(TaggedText) is undefined.
A desk can change what its paths show, for example when a file system is mounted. Desk.same(DeskPath, DeskPath') is about one Desk: a program that relies on same checks again on the new Desk.
